Titre$ K E Y  O F  C L I T O P I L U S   -   ROLF SINGER 1978
Com$ Sydowia 31:235-237, 1978.   -   Adapté par Paul bertéa 19-12-2015
1,0 $ WHY THIS KEY ?
1,0,N$///While the second part of Agaricales keys was prepared, it became obvious that the continuous progress in the exploration of the Agaricales of the world — although in some genera, in some regions, it may seem exasperatingly slow — has made it necessary to caution those who will use the keys of part I (and successively, the keys of earlier publication date) that well defined species should have entered these keys but were omitted at the time because the corresponding literature had not yet come to the attention of the author, or because the author's own research had not been completed at the time the keys had been made ready for the printer. Even so, it would appear that keys reflecting the stand of taxonomic research up to a certain recent date might be valuable and helpful. In addition, it is planned to add supplements to earlier keys, where necessary, at a later date.

1,cs1 $ section Clitopilus. $ Section Clitopilus $s" section Clitopilus"
1,cs2 $ section Scyphoides $ section Scyphoides  $s" section Scyphoides"
1,cs3 $ section Pleurotelloides $ section Pleurotelloides $s" section Pleurotelloides"


cs1,0 $ section Clitopilus

1,0 $ Only one specie $ C. prunulus (Scop, ex Fr.) Quél
1,0,N$///Some mycologists recognize a second species, C. orcella (Bull, ex Fr.), but this does not appear to be specifically distinguishable.



cs2,0 $  section Scyphoides

1,0 $ On horse-manure, often in mushroom houses $
1,0,N$///===> see section Pleurotelloides.
1,2 $ Non on horse-manure, mostly on earth.

2,3 $ Pileus gray.
2,4 $ Pileus white or whitish, rarely with blackening or cream colored center.

3,0 $ Pileus soon bleaching; hyphae of the epicutis with conspicuous granular hyaline incrustations; spores 4.5—7.5 X 3—5.5 µm. $ C. argentinus Sing.
3,0 $ Pileus remaining distinctly gray; elements of the epicutis not incrusted with conspicuous hyaline matter; spores 6—9.5 x 3.5—5 µm. $ C. giovanellae (Bres.) Sing.

4,5 $ Asiatic, tropical species, mostly with strongly (0.4 µm) projecting ridges on the spores which look like longitudinally folded.
4,7 $ European, North and South American and Asiatic species, mostly growing in the temperate zone and subtropics, or else with very fine, scarcely raised longitudinal lines on the spores.

5,0 $ Pileus 30—50 mm broad, with crenate margin. $ C. crispus Pat.
5,6 $ Pileus smaller, thin, with entire margin.

6,0 $ Pileus 5—15 mm broad. Vietnam. $ C. orcelloides Pat. & Demange
6,0 $ Pileus about 25 mm broad. Sri Lanka. $ C. (h)apalus (Berk. & Br.) Petch


7,0 $ South American species growing on rich earth; pileus 1 — 5 mm broad; stipe with a slight or strong bulb at the base; spores 8.3— 10.2x4.3 — 5.8— (6.3) µm. $ C. pusillimus (Speg.) Sing.
7,8 $ Occurring or not in South America; either without basal bulb or with slightly smaller spores or with larger pileus.

8,9 $ Pileus 8—30 mm broad; stipe 0.4—1.2 mm broad, usually short.
8,0 $ Pileus smaller; stipe relatively longer and very thin (0.5 —1 mm); spores 6.7—7.8x3.8 — 4.5 µm. $ C. hrbanovi (Velen.) Sing.
8,0,N$///= C. omphaliformis Joss.

9,0 $ Spores 6—11.5 x 3.7—5.5 µm. $ C. scyphoides (Fr.) Sing.
9,0,N$///= C. cretatus (Bk. & Br. /// ///A form on burned over sod from North Dacota may be conspecific (cf. Singer, Farlowia 2: 557. 1946)./// ///Cf. also "6" above since C. intermedius Romagnesi may be identical with one of the Asiatic species.
9,0 $ Spores < 6—11.5 x 3.7—5.5 µm. $ C. scyphoides var. submicropus (Rick) Sing.



cs3,0 $  section Pleurotelloides

1,0 $ On horse dung, often in mushroom houses; pileus and stipe very irregular in shape and the latter sometimes absent; pileus 10—40 mm broad. $ C. passeckerianus (Pilat) Sing.
1,2 $ Non on horse dung.

2,3 $ Pileus 10—40 mm broad.
2,5 $ Pileus small, up to 11 mm in diameter, only exceptionally larger.


3,4 $ Odor and taste mild, not farinaceous; stipe absent; hyphal walls mostly thin.
3,0 $ Odor and taste strong, more or less farinaceous; stipe short; hyphal walls in part firm and somewhat refringent. On various substrata. $ C. pinsitus (Fr.) Joss, sensu Joss.


4,0 $ On wood in Italy. $ C. rhodophyllus (Bres.) Sing.
4,0 $ On and among mosses over fallen needles in Northern and Central Europe. $ C. scyphoides f. mutilus (Fr.) Sing.
4,0,N$///This is probably an autonomous species.

5,6 $ Hyphae of the pileus with gray-brown to dark fuscous pigment incrustations. From Florida and Mexico south to Uruguay, Argentina, and Chile. : C. incrustatus 
5,7 $ Hyphae of the pileus hyaline-incrusted or not incrusted.

6,0 $ Spores 5—7.3—(8) X 3—4—(5) µm. $ C. incrustatus var. incrustatus Sing.
6,0 $ Spores 7—8.8—(10) X 4—5.7 µm. $ C. incrustatus var. macrosporus Sing.

7,8 $ Hyphae of the hymenophoral trama hyaline in acid medium. 
7,0 $ Hyphae of the hymenophoral trama pink or pinkish red in HC1 mounts. Tropical and subtropical South America. $ C. rhodotrama Sing.

8,0 $ Species growing from Panama south to Argentina on dead wood; hyphae of the epicutis distinctly granular from a hyaline incrustation; pileus at first gray or grayish white becoming paler in age. $ C. argentinus Sing.
8,0,N$///Synonyme : Pleurotus pusillimus Speg.
8,9 $ Hyphae of the epicutis not incrusted; pileus white when young.

9,0 $ Pileus conspicuously sulcate or venose-sulcate. Growing on sticks and trunks in Florida and Argentina. $ C. venososulcatus Sing.
9,0 $ Species of nearly world-wide distribution, growing on earth, rocks, bark, pieces of rotten wood, also on living and dead leaves and stems, on fallen logs and branches (mainly of angiosperms). Pileus smooth or rarely coarsely furrowed or zoned, sometimes somewhat transparently striate. $ C. hobsonii (Berk. & Br.) Orton

